const PATTERN_ACADEMY_4 = {
  "tree-dfs": {
    title: "Pattern 17 — Tree DFS (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Tree DFS is one of the most important patterns for Google/Uber/Amazon interviews. The mistake to avoid is memorizing 'Tree -> recursion.'",
        conclusion: "Instead, understand why DFS works and what state the recursive call represents." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why does Tree DFS exist?",
        body: "             1\n           /   \\\n          2     3\n        /  \\     \\\n       4    5     6\n\nEvery node owns a subtree. For node 2:\n      2\n     / \\\n    4   5\n\nIf the answer for node 2 depends on the answers from 4 and 5, the natural strategy is:\nSolve subtree 4\nSolve subtree 5\nCombine them -> answer for 2\n\nThat's DFS: go deep into a subtree, solve it, return the result to the parent." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Mental model",
        body: "             Node\n            /    \\\n       subtree   subtree\n          |         |\n        solve     solve\n          \\         /\n           \\       /\n            combine\n\nThe recursive call means: 'Give me the answer for this node's subtree.'\nThis is the most important mental model." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "3-4. The three core DFS orders", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Preorder — Node, Left, Right", question: "Process parent before children.",
          diagram: "     1\n    / \\\n   2   3\n\nResult: 1 2 3" },
        { id: "2", title: "Inorder — Left, Node, Right", question: "Especially important for BST: inorder traversal of a valid BST gives sorted order.",
          diagram: "     1\n    / \\\n   2   3\n\nResult: 2 1 3\n\nBST -> Inorder -> Sorted values" },
        { id: "3", title: "Postorder — Left, Right, Node", question: "Extremely important when the parent needs information from its children before it can decide.",
          diagram: "     1\n    / \\\n   2   3\n\nResult: 2 3 1",
          note: "Used for: tree height, tree diameter, Tree DP, delete tree, subtree calculations." }
      ]},
      { type: "chips", heading: "5. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "Think Tree DFS when you see:",
        items: ["Subtree", "Root-to-leaf", "Path", "Tree height/depth", "Diameter", "Count nodes", "Sum of subtree", "Max/min in subtree", "Validate tree property", "Recursive tree relationship"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "6. The core DFS template", dark: true,
        body: "dfs(node):\n\n    if node == null:\n        return base case\n\n    left = dfs(node.left)\n    right = dfs(node.right)\n\n    return combine(node, left, right)\n\nWe're not memorizing a specific algorithm. We're defining: what does dfs(node) return?" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "7. The most important question", tone: "gold",
        body: "Before writing DFS, ask: what should dfs(node) return? Height -> height of this subtree. Sum -> sum of this subtree. Maximum path -> best path information from this node downward. Balanced tree -> height, or an unbalanced signal.",
        conclusion: "This one question will make Tree DP much easier later." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. Example — Maximum Depth",
        body: "       1\n      / \\\n     2   3\n    /\n   4\n\nFor node 4: height = 1\nFor node 2: 1 + max(height(4), height(null))\nFor node 1: 1 + max(height(2), height(3))\n\ndfs(node) = 1 + max(dfs(left), dfs(right))\n\nThis is the basic tree-recursion pattern." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "9. Example — Count Nodes",
        body: "Question: how many nodes are in the tree?\n\nDefine: dfs(node) = number of nodes in this subtree\n\ndfs(node) = 1 + dfs(left) + dfs(right)\n\nAgain: child answer -> parent combines." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. Example — Root-to-Leaf Path",
        body: "Problem: does a root-to-leaf path have a given sum?\n\n       5\n      / \\\n     4   8\n    /\n   11\n\nAt every node, carry remainingSum.\nFor 5 -> 4 -> 11, update:\ntarget -> target-5 -> target-4 -> target-11\n\nSo this is: Tree DFS + State carried from parent to child." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "11. Two types of Tree DFS", items: [
        { label: "Type A — Top Down", body: "Information flows Parent -> Child -> Grandchild. You pass state down. Examples: current path sum, current depth, parent value, path string, constraints. Mental model: what information does the parent give the child?" },
        { label: "Type B — Bottom Up", body: "Information flows Child -> Parent. You get results from children and combine them. Examples: height, diameter, subtree sum, Tree DP, balanced tree. Mental model: what does the child tell the parent?" }
      ], conclusion: null },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "12. This is a huge recognition skill",
        body: "Q1: Does information flow from parent to child?\n    -> Top-down DFS\n\nQ2: Does the parent need answers from its children?\n    -> Bottom-up DFS\n\nQ3: Do I need both?\n    -> Two-state / combined DFS" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "13. Tree Diameter (an important example)",
        body: "Problem: find the longest path between any two nodes.\n\n       1\n      / \\\n     2   3\n    /\n   4\n\nAt node 2, you know:\nlongest downward path through left\nlongest downward path through right\n\nA path passing through 2 can be: leftHeight + rightHeight + 2\n\nSo DFS calculates heights bottom-up.\nThis is: Tree DFS + Bottom-up state + Global answer." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "14. Global answer pattern", tone: "gold",
        body: "Sometimes dfs(node) cannot directly return the final answer. Example: for Diameter, dfs(node) returns the best downward path, but the overall answer may be leftHeight + rightHeight at some node. So we maintain a separate globalAnswer.",
        conclusion: "DFS returns local information -> update global answer -> return information needed by parent. This pattern appears repeatedly in Tree DP." },
      { type: "text", heading: "15. DFS + Backtracking",
        body: "Suppose we want all root-to-leaf paths. Maintain currentPath: when going down, add the node; when returning, remove the node. That's Tree DFS + Backtracking. Mental model: choose -> explore -> undo." },
      { type: "text", heading: "16. DFS + DP (Tree DP)",
        body: "One of the most important combinations for harder interviews. For every node: leftState + rightState -> combine -> nodeState. That's Tree DP. Examples: House Robber III, Maximum independent set on tree, Diameter variants, Minimum camera placement." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "19. When NOT to use Tree DFS", tone: "gold",
        body: "Don't automatically use DFS just because 'it's a tree.' If you need the minimum number of edges (e.g. shortest distance from root to a target with equal-weight edges), BFS is usually more natural and finds minimum depth directly. If the problem explicitly requires level-by-level processing (e.g. average value of each tree level), use BFS. If the tree is extremely deep, recursive DFS can cause stack overflow \u2014 consider Iterative DFS.",
        conclusion: null },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "20. DFS vs BFS",
        body: "Requirement               Prefer\n------------------------  ------\nSubtree calculation       DFS\nRoot-to-leaf path         DFS\nTree DP                   DFS\nHeight                    DFS\nDiameter                  DFS\nMinimum depth             BFS\nLevel order               BFS\nLevel averages            BFS\nNearest node by edges     BFS\n\nThese aren't absolute rules \u2014 a problem may have both DFS and BFS solutions." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "21. All important Tree DFS variations", dark: true,
        body: "                    TREE DFS\n                       |\n          +------------+------------+\n          |            |            |\n      Preorder       Inorder     Postorder\n          |            |            |\n          |           BST        Bottom-up\n          |                         |\n          +------------+------------+\n                       |\n                 State Passing\n                /              \\\n           Top Down          Bottom Up\n               |                  |\n          Path/Depth           Tree DP\n                                   |\n                        +----------+----------+\n                        |          |          |\n                     Height    Diameter    Robber" },
      { type: "chips", heading: "22. Combinations you must know",
        intro: "Tree DFS frequently combines with:",
        items: ["DFS + HashMap", "DFS + Prefix Sum", "DFS + Backtracking", "DFS + DP", "DFS + Stack", "DFS + Greedy", "DFS + BST property"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "23. Complexity",
        body: "For a normal tree traversal:\nTime: O(N) — every node is visited once.\n\nRecursive DFS:\nSpace: O(H) — where H = tree height.\n\nBalanced tree:  H = O(log N)\nWorst-case skewed tree:  H = O(N)\n\nThis distinction is important in interviews." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "24. Interview follow-ups",
        intro: "Common interviewer questions and how to answer them:",
        items: [
          "\"Can you do it iteratively?\" -> Use an explicit Stack.",
          "\"Can you reduce auxiliary space?\" -> Depends on the problem; Morris traversal can help but is advanced and usually not your first answer.",
          "\"Why is it O(N)?\" -> Each node is processed once.",
          "\"What does your recursive function return?\" -> The most important question. Be able to answer in one sentence: 'dfs(node) returns ___ for the subtree rooted at node.'"
        ],
        note: null },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "25. The pattern to burn into muscle memory", dark: true,
        body: "TREE\n |\nWhat information does each node need?\n |\nParent -> child?  OR  Child -> parent?\n |\nDefine dfs(node)\n |\nWhat does dfs(node) return?\n |\nChoose preorder/inorder/postorder\n |\nImplement\n\nThat's much more powerful than memorizing tree solutions." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "26. Representative problems", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Must Master", note: "Maximum Depth of Binary Tree, Same Tree, Invert Binary Tree, Path Sum, Diameter of Binary Tree, Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum, Lowest Common Ancestor, Validate BST." },
        { id: "2", title: "Should Know", note: "Path Sum II, Serialize/Deserialize Binary Tree, Binary Tree Right Side View, Kth Smallest in BST, House Robber III." },
        { id: "3", title: "Advanced", note: "Morris Traversal, Complex Tree DP, Tree rerooting DP." }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "27. Interview recognition card",
        body: "\"For every node, calculate something about its subtree.\"  -> DFS\n\"Find a root-to-leaf path.\"                                -> DFS\n\"Parent depends on children's answers.\"                    -> Bottom-up DFS\n\"Pass current path/depth/sum downward.\"                    -> Top-down DFS\n\"Level-by-level.\"                                          -> BFS\n\"Shortest number of edges.\"                                -> BFS candidate\n\"Tree + optimization/state.\"                               -> Tree DP candidate" },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 3 progress",
        items: ["17. Tree DFS", { label: "18. Tree BFS", done: false }, { label: "19. BST", done: false },
          { label: "20. Tree Path", done: false }, { label: "21. LCA", done: false }, { label: "22. Tree DP", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Tree BFS",
        points: ["Same structure: why it exists, mental model, recognition triggers, invariant, variations, when NOT to use, combinations, complexity, follow-ups", "We'll make the DFS vs BFS decision very clear \u2014 that decision alone eliminates a lot of wrong approaches in tree interviews"] }
    ]
  },

  "tree-bfs": {
    title: "Pattern 18 — Tree BFS (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Tree BFS is the natural counterpart to Tree DFS: process a tree level by level using a queue.",
        conclusion: "If DFS asks 'how deep can I go?', BFS asks 'what is the next level?'" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why does Tree BFS exist?",
        body: "             1\n           /   \\\n          2     3\n         / \\   / \\\n        4   5 6   7\n\nDFS might go: 1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 5 -> 3 -> 6 -> 7\n\nBut sometimes the problem cares about distance from the root:\nLevel 0:       1\nLevel 1:      2  3\nLevel 2:    4 5 6 7\n\nBFS naturally processes exactly this structure." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Mental model — a wave spreading outward",
        body: "             1\n             |\n          +-----+\n          2     3\n          |     |\n       +----+ +----+\n       4    5 6    7\n\nThe wave processes:\ndistance 0 -> distance 1 -> distance 2 -> distance 3\n\nThat's why BFS is excellent for minimum number of edges / levels / distance from root." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3. Why a queue?",
        body: "BFS requires: first discovered -> first processed. That's FIFO.\n\nQueue:\nfront                    back\n  |                         |\n [2] [3] [4] [5] [6] [7]\n\nProcess 2 first because it was discovered first.\nThis ensures we finish one level before moving to the next." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "4. Basic BFS", dark: true,
        body: "Start:\nqueue = [root]\n\nWhile queue isn't empty:\n    node = queue.poll()\n    process(node)\n    add node.left\n    add node.right\n\nThat's the fundamental algorithm." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "5. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "Think Tree BFS when you see:",
        items: ["Level order", "Level by level", "Each depth", "Minimum depth", "Closest node", "Nearest leaf", "Distance from root", "Width of tree", "Right/left view", "Average per level", "Zigzag traversal"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "6. Pattern A — Level Order Traversal",
        body: "       1\n      / \\\n     2   3\n    / \\\n   4   5\n\nOutput:\n[\n  [1],\n  [2,3],\n  [4,5]\n]\n\nBFS naturally produces:\nLevel 0 -> [1]\nLevel 1 -> [2,3]\nLevel 2 -> [4,5]" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "7. The most important BFS technique — process one level", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose queue = [2,3]. We need to know: which nodes belong to this level? Capture size = queue.size(), then process exactly size nodes. Conceptually: size = queue.size(); repeat size times: process one node, add its children. After those size nodes, one level is complete.",
        conclusion: "This technique is extremely important." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. Pattern B — Minimum Depth",
        body: "Problem: find the minimum depth of a binary tree.\n\n        1\n       /\n      2\n     /\n    3\n   /\n  4\n\nDFS can solve this, but BFS has a natural advantage: BFS explores increasing distance, so the first leaf encountered gives the minimum depth.\n\nShortest distance in an unweighted tree -> BFS is a natural choice." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "9. Pattern C — Maximum Width",
        body: "             1\n           /   \\\n          2     3\n         / \\     \\\n        4   5     7\n\nBFS gives us:\nLevel 0 -> 1 node\nLevel 1 -> 2 nodes\nLevel 2 -> 3 nodes\n\nTherefore maximum width = 3.\nMore advanced versions may use positional indices to account for gaps." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. Pattern D — Right Side View",
        body: "Imagine looking at the tree from the right.\n\n             1\n           /   \\\n          2     3\n         /       \\\n        4         7\n\nRight view: 1, 3, 7\n\nBFS processes each level. The last node of each level is visible from the right.\nSo: BFS + last node of each level." },
      { type: "text", heading: "11. Left Side View",
        body: "Same idea, but the first node at every level: 1, 2, 4. So: BFS + first node of each level." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "12. Pattern E — Level Average",
        body: "       1\n      / \\\n     2   4\n    / \\\n   6   8\n\nLevels:\nLevel 0 -> 1\nLevel 1 -> 2, 4\nLevel 2 -> 6, 8\n\nCompute: sum / number of nodes\n\nAgain: BFS + level boundary." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "13. Pattern F — Zigzag Level Order",
        body: "Normal:\n1\n2 3\n4 5 6 7\n\nZigzag:\n1\n3 2\n4 5 6 7\n\nDirection alternates: left->right, right->left, left->right\n\nBFS still provides the levels; we simply change how each level is stored.\nSo: BFS + direction toggle." },
      { type: "text", heading: "14. Pattern G — BFS with Distance",
        body: "Suppose you need to find distance from root to target. Maintain a distance value; each level represents distance + 1. So: BFS + distance state. This becomes extremely important when we reach Graph BFS." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "15. Tree BFS -> Graph BFS (a very important connection)", tone: "gold",
        body: "Tree: Node -> children. Graph: Node -> neighbors. The BFS machinery is almost identical. Tree: queue.add(left), queue.add(right). Graph: queue.add(neighbor). But graph BFS requires a visited set, because graphs can contain cycles.",
        conclusion: "Mastering Tree BFS now makes Graph BFS much easier later." },
      { type: "text", heading: "16. Tree BFS + HashSet",
        body: "Trees normally don't require visited because there are no cycles. But if you're dealing with parent pointers, undirected tree representation, or a tree converted into a graph, then you may need visited. Example: child -> parent can cause you to traverse backwards. So BFS + visited becomes useful." },
      { type: "text", heading: "17. Tree BFS + Queue of State",
        body: "Sometimes the queue doesn't just contain a Node. It contains (Node, depth), or (Node, column), or (Node, state). This is a powerful pattern: BFS can carry state along with each node. Examples: depth, position, column, direction, parent information." },
      { type: "text", heading: "18. BFS + Coordinates",
        body: "For some tree problems, especially vertical traversal, we assign root = column 0, left -> column -1, right -> column +1. Then BFS can process (node, column). This becomes: BFS + HashMap + Coordinate/state. We'll revisit this when we cover advanced tree traversal." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "19. When NOT to use BFS", tone: "gold",
        body: "Don't automatically use BFS just because it's a tree. Subtree calculations (e.g. height of every subtree) usually use DFS. Tree DP usually uses DFS, because you need child answers before computing the parent. Root-to-leaf path construction is usually DFS. Deep recursive structural relationships are often simpler with DFS.",
        conclusion: null },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "20. DFS vs BFS decision",
        body: "                  TREE\n                   |\n          What does the problem ask?\n                   |\n       +-----------+------------+\n       |                        |\n  Subtree / Path / DP      Level / Distance\n       |                        |\n      DFS                      BFS\n\nMore specifically:\n\"Depends on children?\"          -> DFS\n\"Depends on distance/level?\"    -> BFS" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "21. Complexity",
        body: "Every node is processed once: Time = O(N)\n\nQueue can contain an entire level, so: Space = O(W)\nwhere W is maximum tree width.\n\nWorst case: W = O(N)\nFor a balanced binary tree, W is approximately O(N) at the bottom level." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "22. All Tree BFS variations", dark: true,
        body: "                    TREE BFS\n                       |\n        +--------------+--------------+\n        |              |              |\n   Level Order      Distance       View\n        |              |              |\n        |              |        +-----+-----+\n    Zigzag          Min Depth    Left       Right\n        |\n   Level State\n        |\n   +----+-----+\n   |    |     |\n depth column position" },
      { type: "chips", heading: "23. Important combinations",
        intro: "Tree BFS frequently combines with:",
        items: ["BFS + HashMap (vertical traversal, grouping by level/column)", "BFS + Heap (ordering nodes within a level)", "BFS + State (distance, position, constraints)", "BFS + Visited (tree represented as undirected graph)", "BFS + Multi-source (major Graph pattern later)"] },
      { type: "practice", heading: "24. Interview follow-ups",
        intro: "Common interviewer questions and how to answer them:",
        items: [
          "\"Why BFS instead of DFS?\" -> Because the problem is level/distance based. BFS processes nodes in increasing distance from the root, so the first relevant node gives the minimum distance.",
          "\"Can you do it recursively?\" -> Usually yes for many level-order tasks, but iterative BFS is more natural and directly represents the level structure.",
          "\"Why do you capture queue size?\" -> It represents the number of nodes currently belonging to this level, allowing me to process one complete level before moving to the next."
        ],
        note: null },
      { type: "variants", heading: "25. Representative problems", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Must Master", note: "Binary Tree Level Order Traversal, Minimum Depth, Binary Tree Right Side View, Average of Levels, Binary Tree Zigzag Level Order." },
        { id: "2", title: "Should Know", note: "Maximum Width, Vertical Order Traversal, Even/Odd level problems, Distance-related tree problems." },
        { id: "3", title: "Advanced", note: "BFS with complex state, Tree represented as graph + BFS, BFS + coordinate ordering." }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "26. Your tree decision card", dark: true,
        body: "                 TREE\n                  |\n                  v\n        Is it level/distance based?\n             /          \\\n           YES           NO\n            |             |\n           BFS           DFS\n                           |\n                 +---------+---------+\n                 |                   |\n           Parent -> Child       Child -> Parent\n             Top Down            Bottom Up\n                 |                   |\n              Path/State         Tree DP\n\nThis is the decision tree I want you to develop." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 3 progress",
        items: ["17. Tree DFS", "18. Tree BFS", { label: "19. BST", done: false },
          { label: "20. Tree Path", done: false }, { label: "21. LCA", done: false }, { label: "22. Tree DP", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Binary Search Tree (BST)",
        points: ["BST introduces a powerful idea: the tree itself gives you an ordering invariant", "We'll cover: why BST exists, the invariant, recognition triggers, search/insert/delete, inorder, predecessor/successor", "Kth smallest, validation, when BST beats normal DFS, combinations, and all important variations"] }
    ]
  },

  "bst": {
    title: "Pattern 19 — Binary Search Tree (BST) (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "BST problems are not just 'tree problems.' The key advantage of a BST is that the structure itself maintains sorted order.",
        conclusion: "Once you understand that invariant, many BST problems become much easier." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why does BST exist?",
        body: "A normal binary tree doesn't necessarily have an ordering rule. But a Binary Search Tree maintains: left subtree < node < right subtree.\n\n        8\n       / \\\n      3   10\n     / \\\n    1   6\n\nEverything left of 8 is smaller: 1, 3, 6 < 8\nEverything right is larger: 10 > 8\n\nThat's the fundamental BST invariant." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. The mental model",
        body: "Think of every node as a decision:\n\n             8\n           /   \\\n        smaller larger\n\nSearching for 6:\n6 < 8  -> go left\n6 > 3  -> go right\nfound 6\n\nInstead of exploring the entire tree (DFS -> potentially O(N)), BST lets you eliminate half the search space at each decision, if the tree is reasonably balanced." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "3. The BST invariant", tone: "gold",
        body: "Memorize this concept, not a particular implementation: every value in the left subtree is smaller than the node, and every value in the right subtree is larger.",
        conclusion: "This invariant drives: search, insert, delete, validation, kth smallest, predecessor, successor, and range queries." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "4. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "Think 'BST invariant' when you see:",
        items: ["BST", "Sorted binary tree", "Kth smallest", "Kth largest", "Successor", "Predecessor", "Range search", "Ordered tree", "Closest value", "Search value in BST"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5. BST Search",
        body: "        8\n       / \\\n      3   10\n     / \\\n    1   6\n\nSearch 6:\nAt 8: 6 < 8 -> go left\nAt 3: 6 > 3 -> go right\nAt 6: found\n\nWe never visit 10 or 1. That's the power of the ordering invariant." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "6. Complexity",
        body: "Balanced BST:\nSearch = O(log N)\n\nWorst-case skewed BST:\n1\n \\\n  2\n   \\\n    3\n     \\\n      4\n\nNow it behaves like a linked list: Search = O(N)\n\nThis distinction is important: BST does NOT automatically mean O(log N). It depends on tree height." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "7. BST Insert",
        body: "        8\n       / \\\n      3   10\n\nInsert 6:\n6 < 8 -> left\n6 > 3 -> right\n\nResult:\n        8\n       / \\\n      3   10\n       \\\n        6\n\nFollow the invariant until you find the correct empty position." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "8. BST Delete — three cases", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Leaf", diagram: "      3\n     /\n    1\n\nDelete 1: simply remove it." },
        { id: "2", title: "One Child", diagram: "      3          3\n     /    ->    /\n    1          2\n     \\\n      2" },
        { id: "3", title: "Two Children", question: "It has two children, so we need a replacement that preserves BST ordering.",
          diagram: "        8\n       / \\\n      3   10\n     / \\\n    1   6\n\nDelete 3: use inorder successor (smallest in right subtree = 6) or inorder predecessor (largest in left subtree = 1). Replace 3 with one of these, then delete that replacement node." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "9. Inorder Traversal", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is probably the most important BST trick. For a valid BST, inorder traversal produces sorted ascending order.\n\n        8\n       / \\\n      3   10\n     / \\\n    1   6\n\nInorder: 1 -> 3 -> 6 -> 8 -> 10 (sorted)",
        conclusion: "BST + Inorder = Sorted Sequence. Burn this into memory." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. Kth Smallest Element",
        body: "Problem: find the kth smallest value in a BST.\n\nBecause inorder gives sorted order: 1, 3, 6, 8, 10\n\nThe kth element in that sequence is simply the kth smallest.\n\nBST -> Inorder -> kth element. No sorting required." },
      { type: "text", heading: "11. Kth Largest",
        body: "Reverse the idea. Use reverse inorder: Right, Node, Left. This produces descending order. Then the kth element visited is the kth largest." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "12. Recognition trigger",
        intro: "Very high-value recognition pattern:",
        items: ["Kth smallest in BST -> Inorder traversal", "Kth largest in BST -> Reverse inorder"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "13. BST Validation (the common mistake)",
        body: "Problem: determine whether a binary tree is a valid BST.\n\nA common mistake is checking only: node.left < node, node.right > node. That's insufficient.\n\n        8\n       / \\\n      3   10\n         /\n        5\n\nAt node 5: 5 < 10 looks locally valid. But 5 is in the right subtree of 8, so 5 must be > 8. It isn't. The whole tree is invalid." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "14. The correct mental model for validation", tone: "gold",
        body: "Every node has a valid range: node in [minAllowed, maxAllowed]. At the root: (-infinity, +infinity). If node = 8: left subtree gets (-infinity, 8), right subtree gets (8, +infinity). Then recursively tighten the boundaries.",
        conclusion: "This is: DFS + State / Bounds." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "15. BST Successor",
        body: "Successor means the smallest value greater than the current value.\n\n1 3 6 8 10 -> successor of 6 is 8\n\nCase 1: node has a right subtree -> successor = minimum of right subtree.\nCase 2: no right subtree -> walk upward and find the first ancestor for which the node lies in the left subtree." },
      { type: "text", heading: "16. BST Predecessor",
        body: "Opposite of successor: largest value smaller than the current value. If node has a left subtree, predecessor = maximum of left subtree. Otherwise walk upward to find the appropriate ancestor." },
      { type: "text", heading: "17. BST Range Search",
        body: "Problem: find all values between [L, R]. Normal DFS might explore the whole tree, but BST lets us prune. If node < L, everything in the left subtree is also < L, so don't visit it. Similarly if node > R, everything in the right subtree is too large. This is: BST + Pruning. Very important." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "18. BST Closest Value",
        body: "Problem: find the value closest to target.\n\nBST values: 1, 4, 6, 8, 10\ntarget = 7\n\nCompare while traversing: 8 -> difference 1, 6 -> difference 1\n\nThen use the BST ordering to decide which direction to continue. Potentially O(H) instead of scanning all nodes." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "19. BST + Binary Search (a very important conceptual connection)", tone: "gold",
        body: "Array binary search: sorted array -> choose half. BST: ordered tree -> choose left/right. They're based on the same fundamental idea: use ordering to eliminate a large portion of the search space.",
        conclusion: "Binary Search and BST Search are conceptually related." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "20. When NOT to use BST", tone: "gold",
        body: "Don't choose BST simply because the input is a tree. If the tree has no ordering property (a normal Binary Tree), BST-specific tricks don't apply. For example, finding tree diameter uses Tree DFS, not BST. If you need level information, use BFS. If you need subtree DP, use DFS / Tree DP.",
        conclusion: null },
      { type: "chips", heading: "21. Important combinations",
        intro: "BST frequently combines with:",
        items: ["BST + DFS (most BST problems)", "BST + Binary Search (searching/pruning)", "BST + Inorder = Sorted order", "BST + Inorder + Reverse Inorder + Two Pointers (e.g. Two Sum in BST)", "BST + DFS + Bounds (validation)"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "22. All important BST variations", dark: true,
        body: "                       BST\n                        |\n       +----------------+-----------------+\n       |                |                 |\n     Search           Insert            Delete\n       |                                  |\n   O(height)                         3 cases\n                                          |\n                              +-----------+----------+\n                              |           |          |\n                            Leaf      One child  Two children\n\nAnd:\n                       BST\n                        |\n                +-------+--------+\n                |       |        |\n             Inorder  Successor  Range\n                |       |        |\n              Kth     Next      Pruning\n             smallest\n\nAnd the full checklist:\nKth smallest, Kth largest, Validate, Closest value,\nPredecessor, Successor, Range search, Two Sum" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "23. Complexity",
        body: "Let H = tree height.\n\nSearch: O(H)\nInsert: O(H)\nDelete: O(H)\n\nBalanced BST: H = O(log N)\nWorst case:   H = O(N)\n\nInorder traversal: O(N)" },
      { type: "practice", heading: "24. Interview follow-ups",
        intro: "Common interviewer questions and how to answer them:",
        items: [
          "\"Why is BST search O(log N)?\" -> Only if the tree is balanced: each decision eliminates one subtree, so complexity is proportional to tree height, which is O(log N) for a balanced BST.",
          "\"How do you validate a BST?\" -> Don't say 'check left < node and right > node.' Say: 'I maintain valid lower and upper bounds for each subtree.'",
          "\"How do you find kth smallest?\" -> 'Inorder traversal of a BST produces sorted ascending order, so the kth visited node is the kth smallest.'"
        ],
        note: null },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "25. BST Recognition Card", dark: true,
        body: "BST\n  |\nOrdering invariant\n  |\nCan I eliminate a subtree?\n  |\n YES\n  |\nUse BST property\n\nSpecific triggers:\nKth smallest   -> Inorder\nKth largest    -> Reverse inorder\nSearch         -> BST traversal\nValidate       -> Bounds\nSuccessor      -> Right subtree / ancestor\nPredecessor    -> Left subtree / ancestor\nRange          -> Pruning\nClosest        -> BST traversal" },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 3 progress",
        items: ["17. Tree DFS", "18. Tree BFS", "19. BST", { label: "20. Tree Path", done: false }, { label: "21. LCA", done: false }, { label: "22. Tree DP", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Tree Path Problems",
        points: ["This is a family, not just one algorithm", "Root->Leaf, Root->Any Node, Any Node->Any Node, Maximum Path, Path Sum, Count Paths", "Path + Prefix Sum, Path + HashMap, Path + Backtracking, Path + Tree DP", "This is where you'll start seeing combinations of patterns, exactly what happens in Google/Uber/Amazon interviews"] }
    ]
  },

  "tree-path": {
    title: "Pattern 20 — Tree Path Problems (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is an important family because interviewers often don't ask 'Do a DFS.' Instead, they'll give you a path requirement, and you need to figure out what information the DFS should carry or return.",
        conclusion: "A tree path problem asks you to reason about a sequence of connected nodes, and your main job is to define what information a path needs." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why does the Tree Path pattern exist?",
        body: "             5\n           /   \\\n          4     8\n         / \\   / \\\n        1   3 7   9\n\nThere are many possible paths:\n5 -> 4 -> 1\n5 -> 4 -> 3\n5 -> 8 -> 7\n5 -> 8 -> 9\n\nThe interviewer might ask: does a path sum to X? What is the maximum path sum? How many paths sum to X? What is the longest path? Return all root-to-leaf paths.\n\nThese sound different, but they're often solved by: Tree DFS + Path state." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "2. The first question you must ask", tone: "gold",
        body: "When you see a path problem: what exactly is a 'valid path'? There are several possibilities. Type 1: Root -> Leaf. Type 2: Root -> Any Node. Type 3: Any Node -> Any Node. Type 4: Any downward path.",
        conclusion: "These are not the same problem. This distinction is extremely important." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3. Path Type #1 — Root -> Leaf",
        body: "       5\n      / \\\n     4   8\n    /\n   1\n\nA valid path must START at root and END at leaf.\n\nExample: 5 -> 4 -> 1 is valid.\nBut 5 -> 4 is not necessarily valid if 4 isn't a leaf." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "4. Pattern — Path Sum",
        body: "Problem: does a root-to-leaf path have sum target?\n\ntarget = 10\nPath: 5 -> 4 -> 1\nSum: 5 + 4 + 1 = 10\n\nAt each DFS step, carry remainingSum.\nStart: 10\nAfter 5: 5\nAfter 4: 1\nAt leaf: remaining == node.value -> success." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "5. Recognition trigger",
        intro: "If the problem says:",
        items: ["root-to-leaf", "path sum", "target sum"],
        note: "Think: DFS + path state." },
      { type: "text", heading: "6. Path Type #2 — Root -> Any Node",
        body: "Now suppose the path can stop anywhere. Example: 5 -> 4. If stopping at 4 is allowed, then you don't need to wait for a leaf. The condition is evaluated at every node. So Root -> Any Node is generally easier than root-to-leaf." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "7. Path Type #3 — Any Node -> Any Node",
        body: "This is where things become more interesting.\n\n             5\n           /   \\\n          4     8\n         /     / \\\n        1     7   9\n\nA valid path might be: 1 -> 4 -> 5 -> 8 -> 9\n\nNotice: it doesn't have to start at the root, and it can travel from one subtree through the parent into another subtree.\n\nThis is the pattern behind: Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. Why Maximum Path Sum is different",
        body: "Suppose at node 5:\nleft contribution = 4\nright contribution = 8\n\nA path passing through 5 can be: 4 -> 5 -> 8, value = 4 + 5 + 8\n\nBut what should dfs(5) return to its parent? It cannot return both branches. A path going upward through the parent can only use 5 -> one side. It cannot branch.\n\nThis is a critical Tree DP insight." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "9. The two different values", items: [
        { label: "Value returned to parent", body: "The best downward path. Only one branch can be used." },
        { label: "Global answer", body: "left contribution + node + right contribution. Both branches can be used." }
      ], conclusion: "This pattern appears repeatedly in tree problems." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. This is the 'Return vs Global' pattern", dark: true,
        body: "               node\n              /    \\\n           left    right\n             \\      /\n              \\    /\n              node\n                |\n        +-------+--------+\n        v                v\n    Return to parent   Global answer\n    ONE branch         BOTH branches\n\nThis distinction is extremely important." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "11. Path Type #4 — Count Paths",
        body: "Now the question changes: how many downward paths have sum K?\n\n             10\n            /  \\\n           5   -3\n          / \\\n         3   2\n\nA path doesn't necessarily need to start at root. This makes simple remainingSum DFS insufficient. We need something more powerful." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "12. Path + Prefix Sum + HashMap", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is a very important combination. Suppose the current path has prefixSum = S. We want a previous prefix: previousSum = S - K, because S - (S-K) = K. So maintain HashMap<prefixSum, frequency>.",
        conclusion: "This is exactly the same mathematical idea as Subarray Sum Equals K, but applied to a tree path. That's a very important interview connection." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "13. Array vs Tree connection", items: [
        { label: "Array", body: "prefix sum + HashMap" },
        { label: "Tree", body: "DFS + prefix sum + HashMap" }
      ], conclusion: "The underlying mathematical trick is the same. This is exactly the kind of pattern transfer you need for FAANG interviews." },
      { type: "text", heading: "14. Path + Backtracking",
        body: "Suppose we need: return all root-to-leaf paths. Maintain currentPath. When entering: path.add(node). When leaving: path.remove(last). That's DFS + Backtracking. Mental model: Choose -> Explore -> Undo." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "15. Why backtracking is necessary",
        body: "       1\n      / \\\n     2   3\n\nPath while exploring left: [1,2]\nAfter finishing 2, you must restore: [1]\nbefore exploring 3.\n\nOtherwise you'd incorrectly get: [1,2,3], which isn't a valid path." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "16. Path Type #5 — Longest Path / Diameter",
        body: "A path doesn't necessarily have to care about values. It may care about number of edges/nodes. This leads to Tree Diameter.\n\nAt every node:\nlongest downward path through left\nlongest downward path through right\n\nThen: candidate = left + right + edges through node\n\nAgain: Tree DFS + Bottom-up state + Global answer." },
      { type: "text", heading: "17. Path Type #6 — Path With Constraints",
        body: "Examples: maximum path with certain values, path containing no duplicates, path satisfying a threshold, longest increasing path. Now DFS may carry: previousValue, currentLength, constraint state. This becomes: DFS + State." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "18. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "When you see: Path, Root-to-leaf, Path sum, Maximum path, Longest path, Number of paths, All paths, Downward path — ask four questions:",
        items: ["Q1: Where can the path start? (Root? Any node?)", "Q2: Where can it end? (Leaf? Any node?)", "Q3: Can it pass through a node and use both children? YES -> global path/diameter style, NO -> downward path", "Q4: What state does the path need? (sum? length? max? set? frequency?)"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "19. The most important path decision", dark: true,
        body: "                    TREE PATH\n                        |\n             Where can it start?\n                 /           \\\n              Root          Any node\n               |               |\n               v               v\n          Top-down          Global path\n               |               |\n          Path state       Tree DP / DFS\n\nThen:\nCan path use BOTH left and right subtrees at a node?\n\n  YES -> Global path / Diameter / Max Path\n  NO  -> Downward path" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "20. When NOT to use this pattern", tone: "gold",
        body: "Don't use Tree Path techniques if the problem is primarily: Level-based (use BFS), BST ordering (use the BST invariant), Graph with cycles (use Graph DFS/BFS + visited), or Pure subtree calculation (regular Tree DFS may be enough).",
        conclusion: null },
      { type: "chips", heading: "21. Important combinations",
        intro: "This is where this pattern becomes powerful:",
        items: ["Tree Path + DFS (basic foundation)", "Tree Path + Backtracking (all paths)", "Tree Path + Prefix Sum + HashMap (count paths with sum K)", "Tree Path + DP (maximum path, diameter)", "Tree Path + Set (path uniqueness / constraints)", "Tree Path + Two Pointers (less common, specialized state problems)"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "22. All important variations", dark: true,
        body: "                     TREE PATH\n                         |\n       +-----------------+-----------------+\n       |                 |                 |\n  Root -> Leaf       Root -> Any       Any -> Any\n       |                 |                 |\n       v                 v                 v\n Path Sum           Path State        Max Path\n All Paths                            Diameter\n       |                                  |\n       v                                  v\nBacktracking                         Tree DP\n\nAnd:\n                  PATH + STATE\n                      |\n        +-------------+-------------+\n        v             v             v\n       Sum          Length       Constraint\n        |\n        v\nPrefix Sum + HashMap" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "23. Complexity",
        body: "Most basic tree path DFS: Time = O(N)\n\nBut combinations can increase complexity. For example, if you explicitly construct every root-to-leaf path, the output itself can be large.\n\nFor prefix-sum HashMap:\nTime: O(N)\nSpace: O(H)\nassuming the prefix map is maintained along the DFS path." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "24. Interview follow-ups",
        intro: "Common interviewer questions and how to answer them:",
        items: [
          "\"Why can't your DFS return the maximum path?\" -> For maximum path sum: the path returned to the parent can only extend through one child, while the global maximum path may pass through both children. So I return the best downward contribution and update a global maximum using both sides.",
          "\"Why use a HashMap?\" -> For path-sum counting: I store frequencies of previous prefix sums so that for current sum S, I can immediately determine how many earlier prefixes equal S-K.",
          "\"Why backtrack?\" -> The path represents the current DFS branch, so I remove the node when returning to the parent to restore the previous path state."
        ],
        note: null },
      { type: "variants", heading: "25. Representative problems", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Must Master", note: "Path Sum, Path Sum II, Diameter of Binary Tree, Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum, Path Sum III, Sum Root to Leaf Numbers." },
        { id: "2", title: "Should Know", note: "Longest Univalue Path, Binary Tree Paths, Maximum Difference Between Node and Ancestor, Pseudo-Palindromic Paths." },
        { id: "3", title: "Advanced", note: "Complex path + state problems, Tree path + DP, Rerooting-style path problems." }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "26. Your Tree Path Recognition Card", dark: true,
        body: "When you see PATH, ask:\n\n1. Where does it start?\n        -> Root / Any node\n\n2. Where does it end?\n        -> Leaf / Any node\n\n3. Can it use both children?\n        -> Yes: Global path\n        -> No: Downward path\n\n4. What must I track?\n        -> Sum / Length / Max / State\n\n5. Do I need all paths?\n        -> Backtracking\n\n6. Count paths with a sum?\n        -> Prefix Sum + HashMap\n\nThis decision tree is far more valuable than memorizing individual path problems." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 3 progress",
        items: ["17. Tree DFS", "18. Tree BFS", "19. BST", "20. Tree Path", { label: "21. LCA", done: false }, { label: "22. Tree DP", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA)",
        points: ["LCA is another important tree pattern — we'll learn the full family, not just one LCA solution", "Binary Tree LCA, BST LCA, LCA with Parent Pointers, LCA of Multiple Nodes, LCA + Distance, LCA + Binary Lifting", "And, importantly, why LCA works, so you can derive the solution instead of memorizing it"] }
    ]
  },

  "lca": {
    title: "Pattern 21 — Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA) (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "LCA is a classic tree pattern, but the important part is not memorizing the code.",
        conclusion: "The key idea is: find the lowest node in the tree that is an ancestor of two or more target nodes." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why does LCA exist?",
        body: "             1\n           /   \\\n          2     3\n         / \\   / \\\n        4   5 6   7\n\nWhat's the LCA of 4 and 5?\n4 -> 2 -> 5\n     ^\n    LCA\nAnswer: 2\n\nLCA of 4 and 6?\n4 -> 2 -> 1 -> 3 -> 6\n         ^\n        LCA\nAnswer: 1" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. What does 'lowest' mean?",
        body: "An ancestor is any node above another node.\n\nFor 4:\n1 -> 2 -> 4\nAncestors of 4: 1, 2\n\nFor 5:\n1 -> 2 -> 5\nAncestors of 5: 1, 2\n\nThe lowest common ancestor is 2, because it's closer to the targets than 1." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "3. Mental model", tone: "gold",
        body: "Think of two nodes traveling upward: 4 -> 2 -> 1, and 5 -> 2 -> 1. They first meet at 2. That's the LCA.",
        conclusion: "Another mental model: LCA is the point where two root-to-node paths diverge. Path to 4: 1 -> 2 -> 4. Path to 5: 1 -> 2 -> 5. Common prefix: 1 -> 2. Last common node: 2." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "4. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "Think LCA when you see:",
        items: ["Lowest common ancestor", "Common ancestor", "Closest common parent", "Nearest common ancestor", "Two nodes' common parent", "Distance between two tree nodes"],
        note: "Also indirectly: find distance/path between two nodes. Often LCA + depth can solve it." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5. Binary Tree LCA",
        body: "             1\n           /   \\\n          2     3\n         / \\\n        4   5\n\nFind LCA of 4 and 5.\n\nThe important observation: if one target is found in the left subtree and the other in the right subtree, the current node is their LCA.\n\nAt node 2: 4 -> left, 5 -> right. Therefore LCA = 2." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "6. The recursive mental model", tone: "gold",
        body: "Define dfs(node) as: 'Does this subtree contain one of my target nodes, and if so, which relevant node/LCA does it contain?' At each node, compute leftResult and rightResult.",
        conclusion: "Case 1: current node is p or q -> return current node. Case 2: both left and right return something -> current node = LCA. Case 3: only one side returns something -> pass that result upward." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "7. The three cases", items: [
        { id: "A", title: "X == p or X == q", question: "Return X." },
        { id: "B", title: "Left contains target AND right contains target", question: "Then X = LCA. This is the heart of the recursive solution." },
        { id: "C", title: "Only one side contains a target", question: "Return that side's result." }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. Example",
        body: "             1\n           /   \\\n          2     3\n         / \\\n        4   5\n\nFind LCA(4,5).\n\nAt 1: left -> something, right -> null. So return left result.\n\nAt 2: left -> 4, right -> 5. Both exist. Therefore 2 = LCA. Return 2 upward." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "9. Important edge case",
        body: "What if one node is an ancestor of the other?\n\n       1\n      /\n     2\n    /\n   4\n\nLCA(2,4) is 2, because 2 itself is an ancestor of 4.\n\nThat's why the base case: if node == p or node == q, return node — is important." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "10. BST LCA", tone: "gold",
        body: "BST gives us extra information.\n\n             8\n           /   \\\n          3     10\n         / \\\n        1   6\n\nFind LCA(1,6).\n\nAt 8: 1 < 8 and 6 < 8, both are left, so go left.\nAt 3: 1 < 3 and 6 > 3, they split around 3.",
        conclusion: "Therefore LCA = 3." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "11. BST LCA mental model",
        body: "Because of the BST invariant (left < node < right), at the current node:\n\nBoth smaller (p < node and q < node) -> go left\nBoth larger (p > node and q > node) -> go right\nThey split (p < node < q) -> current node is LCA" },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "12. Binary Tree LCA vs BST LCA", items: [
        { label: "Normal Binary Tree", body: "No ordering. Need DFS. Potentially O(N)." },
        { label: "BST", body: "Ordering exists. Can use the BST property. Potentially O(H), where H is tree height. Balanced: O(log N)." }
      ], conclusion: "This distinction is important." },
      { type: "text", heading: "13. LCA with Parent Pointers",
        body: "Suppose each node has a parent pointer. Then 4 -> 2 -> 1 and 5 -> 2 -> 1. You can find ancestors of one node: Set = {4,2,1}. Then walk upward from the other node: 5 -> 2. 2 is the first common ancestor. So: Parent pointers + HashSet is one solution." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. LCA Using Depth Alignment",
        body: "Suppose depth(p) = 10, depth(q) = 6.\n\nBring the deeper node up (p moves up 4 times) until both have equal depth.\n\nThen move both upward together: p moves, q moves, until p == q.\n\nThat's the LCA. This is especially useful when parent pointers are available." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "15. LCA + Distance", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is a very important combination. Suppose depth(a) = 5, depth(b) = 7, depth(LCA) = 2. Distance between a and b: depth(a) + depth(b) - 2 * depth(LCA) = 5 + 7 - 2(2) = 8.",
        conclusion: "Distance = depth(a) + depth(b) - 2 x depth(LCA). This is extremely useful in advanced tree/graph problems." },
      { type: "text", heading: "16. LCA + Path",
        body: "If you need the path a -> ... -> LCA -> ... -> b, you can combine LCA + parent/depth information, or reconstruct paths using DFS." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "17. LCA of Multiple Nodes",
        body: "Suppose nodes = [4,5,6]. You can repeatedly apply: LCA(LCA(4,5), 6).\n\nFor LCA(4,5) = 2, then LCA(2,6) = 1.\n\nTherefore LCA(4,5,6) = 1. This is a useful extension." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "18. LCA + Binary Lifting", tone: "gold",
        body: "For a single LCA query, normal DFS is enough. But imagine N = 100,000 and Queries = 100,000, and you need thousands of LCA queries. Running DFS for every query can be too expensive.\n\nThen we preprocess ancestors: parent[node][0] = immediate parent, parent[node][1] = 2^1 ancestor, parent[node][2] = 2^2 ancestor, and so on. This is called Binary Lifting.",
        conclusion: "Then each query can be answered in approximately O(log N) after preprocessing. This is an advanced pattern, not something you need to master immediately." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "19. Why Binary Lifting Exists",
        body: "Moving one parent at a time to climb 100,000 levels could require O(N).\n\nInstead, jump: 2^0, 2^1, 2^2, 2^3, ... like binary search.\n\nFor example, to move up 13 levels: 13 = 8 + 4 + 1.\n\nSo jump 8, then 4, then 1 — rather than 13 individual steps." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "20. When NOT to use LCA", tone: "gold",
        body: "Don't use LCA just because two nodes are mentioned. If the problem asks 'find the path from root to a node', use DFS. If 'find shortest path in a general graph', use BFS / Dijkstra depending on edge weights. If 'find common ancestor in a BST', use the BST property directly. If it's only one simple LCA query, don't overengineer with Binary Lifting.",
        conclusion: null },
      { type: "chips", heading: "21. Combinations",
        intro: "LCA often combines with:",
        items: ["LCA + DFS", "LCA + BST", "LCA + Depth", "LCA + Parent pointers", "LCA + Binary Lifting", "LCA + Distance", "LCA + Path reconstruction"],
        note: "The most important: LCA + Depth = Distance." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "22. All important LCA variations", dark: true,
        body: "                         LCA\n                          |\n          +---------------+----------------+\n          |               |                |\n    Binary Tree          BST         Parent Pointers\n          |               |                |\n         DFS          Ordering         HashSet/\n          |                                Depth\n          v\n     +----+----+\n     |         |\n   Two       Multiple\n  Nodes       Nodes\n                |\n                v\n          LCA(LCA(a,b),c)\n\nAdvanced:\n                LCA\n                 |\n                 v\n          Binary Lifting\n                 |\n                 v\n          Many LCA Queries" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "23. Complexity",
        body: "Normal Binary Tree — single LCA:\nTime: O(N)\nSpace: O(H)\n\nBST (with iterative traversal):\nTime: O(H)\nSpace: O(1)\n\nBinary Lifting:\nPreprocessing: O(N log N)\nQuery: O(log N)\nSpace: O(N log N)" },
      { type: "practice", heading: "24. Interview follow-ups",
        intro: "Common interviewer questions and how to answer them:",
        items: [
          "\"Why is the current node the LCA when both sides return?\" -> Because one target exists in the left subtree and the other in the right subtree, so the current node is their lowest common ancestor.",
          "\"What if one target is the ancestor?\" -> Return the target itself.",
          "\"Can you optimize for many queries?\" -> Use Binary Lifting or another LCA preprocessing technique.",
          "\"How do you find distance?\" -> depth(a) + depth(b) - 2 x depth(LCA)."
        ],
        note: null },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "25. Recognition Card", dark: true,
        body: "When you see: Two nodes + common ancestor\nThink: LCA\n\nThen:\nIs it BST?\n  YES -> BST ordering\n  NO  -> DFS-based LCA\n\nIf many queries? Think: Binary Lifting.\nIf need distance? Think: LCA + depth." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 3 progress",
        items: ["17. Tree DFS", "18. Tree BFS", "19. BST", "20. Tree Path", "21. LCA", { label: "22. Tree DP", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Tree DP",
        points: ["This is particularly important because DP is one of the areas you've said you're struggling with — we'll slow down and build from first principles", "What is DP? -> Why tree structure creates DP -> Define state -> What does dfs(node) return? -> Child states -> Transition -> Base case", "Top-down vs bottom-up -> Choose/Don't Choose -> Tree DP + global answer -> Tree DP variations", "Once you understand Tree DP this way, House Robber III, Maximum Independent Set on Tree, Tree Diameter variants stop looking like unrelated problems"] }
    ]
  },

  "tree-dp": {
    title: "Pattern 22 — Tree DP (Must Master)",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is one of the most important patterns for you because it connects Trees + DFS + Dynamic Programming. This is where I want you to stop thinking 'I need to memorize this DP solution.'",
        conclusion: "Instead: a node's answer can be calculated from the answers of its children. That is the foundation of Tree DP." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why does Tree DP exist?",
        body: "             1\n           /   \\\n          2     3\n         / \\\n        4   5\n\nSuppose I ask: what is the maximum value I can obtain from the subtree rooted at 2?\n\nThe answer depends on: subtree of 4, subtree of 5\n\nSo: answer(2) -> answer(4) + answer(5)\n\nThis is exactly the same principle as normal DP: solve smaller subproblems, reuse their answers, build the larger answer. The tree naturally gives us the subproblems." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. The mental model", dark: true,
        body: "                    Node\n                  /      \\\n           left subtree   right subtree\n                |              |\n           solve once      solve once\n                \\              /\n                 \\            /\n                  \\          /\n                   combine\n                      |\n                  node state\n\nSo Tree DP is basically: DFS + DP state + transition." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "3. The most important question", tone: "gold",
        body: "When solving Tree DP, ask: 'What does dp(node) mean?' This is the most important question. Height: dp(node) = height of subtree rooted at node. Maximum sum: dp(node) = maximum sum obtainable from this subtree.",
        conclusion: "House Robber needs more than one value: dp(node) = best answer if node is selected, and best answer if node is not selected. This leads to multi-state Tree DP." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "4. Tree DP has a standard structure", dark: true,
        body: "Almost every Tree DP can be thought of as:\n1. Define state\n2. Solve children\n3. Combine child states\n4. Calculate current node state\n5. Return state to parent\n\nIn code:\nState dfs(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) {\n        return baseState;\n    }\n    State left = dfs(node.left);\n    State right = dfs(node.right);\n    return combine(node, left, right);\n}\n\nThe difficult part isn't the recursion. The difficult part is: what should State contain?" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5. Type 1 — Single-State Tree DP",
        body: "Simplest example: Maximum depth.\n\nDefine: dp(node) = height of subtree\n\nThen: dp(node) = 1 + max(dp(left), dp(right))\n\nThis is Tree DP. You have: child states -> transition -> parent state." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "6. Type 2 — Sum DP",
        body: "Suppose: calculate the sum of all values in a subtree.\n\nDefine: dp(node) = subtree sum\n\nTransition: dp(node) = node.val + dp(left) + dp(right)\n\nAgain: left answer + right answer + current." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "7. Type 3 — Choose / Don't Choose", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is one of the most important Tree DP patterns. Suppose: select nodes so that no parent and child are both selected, maximizing total value.\n\n             3\n           /   \\\n          4     5\n         / \\\n        1   3\n\nIf you choose 3, you cannot choose 4 or 5. If you don't choose 3, you may choose 4 and/or 5.",
        conclusion: "So each node needs two states." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. Two-State DP",
        body: "For each node:\ndp[node][0] = best answer if node is NOT selected\ndp[node][1] = best answer if node IS selected\n\nThis is the classic House Robber III structure." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "9. Transition", dark: true,
        body: "Suppose: node = X, left = L, right = R\n\nIf we choose X, we cannot choose its children:\nchoose(X) = X.value + don'tChoose(L) + don'tChoose(R)\n\nIf we don't choose X, children can either be chosen or not:\ndon'tChoose(X) = max(choose(L), don'tChoose(L)) + max(choose(R), don'tChoose(R))\n\nFinally:\nanswer = max(choose(root), don'tChoose(root))\n\nThis is a very important pattern." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "10. The recognition trigger",
        intro: "When you see: choose or don't choose nodes under a constraint, think: Tree DP with multiple states. Typical triggers:",
        items: ["Can't choose adjacent nodes", "Parent-child restriction", "Select maximum nodes/value", "Minimum cameras", "Place resources", "Cover tree"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "11. Why greedy doesn't work here",
        body: "             10\n           /    \\\n          5      5\n\nA greedy approach might say: pick the largest current node. But choosing 10 prevents choosing its children.\n\nSometimes: children total > parent. So you need to compare alternatives.\n\nThat's why Choose vs Don't choose becomes DP." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "12. Type 4 — Tree DP + Global Answer", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is another critical pattern. Consider Tree Diameter. At every node: leftHeight, rightHeight. A path passing through the current node can be leftHeight + rightHeight. But the value returned to the parent is only 1 + max(leftHeight, rightHeight).",
        conclusion: "dfs(node) returns one value; the global answer can use both children. This gives us: Return State vs Global Answer." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "13. Why can't we return both branches?",
        body: "             X\n            / \\\n           A   B\n\nA path going from X upward to its parent can only go through X -> A or X -> B. It cannot go A -> X -> B -> parent, because that branches.\n\nTherefore: return to parent = ONE branch.\n\nBut a complete path passing through X can use: left + X + right.\n\nSo: local return != global answer. This is a major Tree DP concept." },
      { type: "text", heading: "14. Type 5 — Tree DP With State Passed Down",
        body: "Not all tree DP is purely bottom-up. Sometimes the state flows parent -> child. Example: find the longest path satisfying some constraint based on the parent's value. You might pass previousValue and currentLength down the tree. This is closer to Top-down DP." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "15. Top-Down vs Bottom-Up", items: [
        { label: "Bottom-Up", body: "children -> parent. Ask: what do my children tell me? Common for: Height, Diameter, House Robber III, Subtree optimization." },
        { label: "Top-Down", body: "parent -> child. Ask: what information does the parent give me? Common for: Path constraints, Depth, Prefix state, Root-to-node optimization." }
      ], conclusion: "This distinction is extremely important." },
      { type: "text", heading: "16. Sometimes You Need Both",
        body: "Some advanced tree problems require information from parent + information from children. This leads to Rerooting DP. Example: calculate an answer assuming every node becomes the root. Naively, running DFS from every node could be O(N^2). Rerooting DP can often reduce this dramatically. This is an advanced Tree DP pattern." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "17. Type 6 — Tree DP + Rerooting", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose you have a chain A - B - C, and you want some answer for root = A, root = B, root = C. Instead of recomputing the whole tree each time, you reuse information when moving the root: root A -> root B -> root C.",
        conclusion: "This is Rerooting DP. You don't need to master this immediately, but you should know it exists." },
      { type: "text", heading: "18. Type 7 — Tree DP + Counting",
        body: "Suppose: count the number of valid configurations of a tree. For each node, you might maintain dp[node][state], where state represents something like selected/not selected, covered/not covered. Then combine children. This appears in: vertex cover, independent set, tree coloring, camera placement, tree matching." },
      { type: "text", heading: "19. Tree DP + Greedy",
        body: "Some Tree DP problems have a greedy-looking decision. Example: place minimum cameras so every node is monitored. You can define states such as 0 = not covered, 1 = covered, 2 = camera. Then derive transitions from children. This is: Tree DP + State Machine." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "20. Tree DP + State Machine", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is a very useful way to think about advanced DP. Instead of dp[node], you might have dp[node][state]. For example: state 0 -> node not selected, state 1 -> node selected. Or: state 0 -> not covered, state 1 -> covered, state 2 -> camera.",
        conclusion: "The tree structure determines the transitions." },
      { type: "text", heading: "21. Tree DP + HashMap",
        body: "Sometimes each node has a large or variable state. Then you might use HashMap<State, Answer>. This is less common for basic interview problems but useful for advanced state compression." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "22. Tree DP + Prefix/Suffix",
        body: "Advanced Tree DP can also combine information from: left subtree, right subtree, parent side. This often appears in rerooting problems.\n\n             node\n       +------+------+\n      left          right\n       |              |\n   information    information\n       \\              /\n        \\            /\n         node answer" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "23. When NOT to use Tree DP", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is important. Don't use Tree DP simply because the input is a tree. Simple traversal (visiting every node) -> use DFS/BFS, no DP needed. Level order -> use BFS. BST search -> use the BST invariant. Simple root-to-leaf existence -> basic DFS may be enough.",
        conclusion: "DP becomes necessary when the same type of subproblem needs to be solved recursively and the parent's answer depends on those subproblem answers." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "24. Tree DFS vs Tree DP", items: [
        { label: "Tree DFS", body: "The primary concern: how do I traverse the tree?" },
        { label: "Tree DP", body: "The primary concern: what information should each subtree return?" }
      ], conclusion: "DFS = traversal mechanism. DP = state + reuse/combination. Tree DP usually uses DFS, but DFS itself isn't automatically DP." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "25. Tree DP recognition decision tree", dark: true,
        body: "                TREE\n                  |\n       Does parent depend on\n       child answers?\n              /     \\\n            NO       YES\n            |         |\n       DFS/BFS      Tree DP\n                       |\n                 What state?\n                       |\n             +---------+---------+\n             |         |         |\n          Single    Multiple   Parent\n           state     states     state\n\nIf multiple states: dp[node][state]" },
      { type: "practice", heading: "26. The most important Tree DP questions",
        intro: "Before coding, ask these five questions:",
        items: [
          "1. What does my state mean? (dp[node] = ?)",
          "2. What information do I need from children? (leftState, rightState)",
          "3. How do I combine them? (transition)",
          "4. Does the parent impose a restriction? If yes: multiple states.",
          "5. Is there a global answer separate from what I return? If yes: return state + global answer."
        ],
        note: "This is the Tree DP checklist I want you to use in interviews." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "27. All important Tree DP variations", dark: true,
        body: "                         TREE DP\n                            |\n             +--------------+---------------+\n             |              |               |\n        Single State    Multi-State     Top Down\n             |              |               |\n        +----+----+      Choose/Don't    Parent ->\n        |    |    |         Choose         Child\n      Height Sum Path\n             |              |\n             v              v\n          Diameter       House Robber\n\nAdvanced:\n                     TREE DP\n                        |\n              +---------+---------+\n              |                   |\n          Bottom Up           Rerooting\n              |                   |\n              v                   v\n        Child -> Parent      Every node as root\n\nAnd:\n                     TREE DP\n                        |\n            +-----------+-----------+\n            |           |           |\n        Optimization   Counting   State Machine\n            |                       |\n            v                       v\n        Max/Min                Select/Cover/etc." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "28. Representative problems", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Must Master", note: "House Robber III, Diameter of Binary Tree, Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum, Balanced Binary Tree." },
        { id: "2", title: "Should Know", note: "Binary Tree Cameras, Maximum Independent Set on Tree, Tree vertex cover, Longest Univalue Path." },
        { id: "3", title: "Advanced", note: "Rerooting DP, Tree DP with complex states, Tree matching, DP with large state spaces." }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "29. Complexity",
        body: "For standard Tree DP: each node is processed once.\nTherefore: Time = O(N)\n\nIf each node has a constant number of states: Space = O(N)\nor recursion stack: O(H), depending on implementation.\n\nRerooting and more advanced states can have different complexity." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "30. Interview follow-ups",
        intro: "Common interviewer questions and how to answer them:",
        items: [
          "\"Why is this DP?\" -> The subtree rooted at each node is an independent subproblem, and the parent's state can be computed from the already-computed child states.",
          "\"Why two states?\" -> The constraint changes the valid choices depending on whether the current node is selected, so one value isn't sufficient to represent the subtree.",
          "\"Why bottom-up?\" -> The parent cannot compute its state until it knows the states of its children.",
          "\"What does your DFS return?\" -> You should answer this before writing code."
        ],
        note: null },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "31. The most important mental model", dark: true,
        body: "Don't think: 'Tree DP = complicated recursion'\n\nThink:\n             TREE\n               |\n          Every subtree\n          = subproblem\n               |\n         Define state\n               |\n        Solve children\n               |\n          Transition\n               |\n        Return state\n\nAnd when a constraint creates choices:\n             Node\n              |\n       +------+------+\n       |             |\n    Choose       Don't Choose\n       |             |\n    State 1        State 0\n\nThat is Tree DP." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 3 — COMPLETE",
        items: ["17. Tree DFS", "18. Tree BFS", "19. BST", "20. Tree Path", "21. LCA", "22. Tree DP"] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Tier 4: Graphs (starting with Graph DFS)",
        points: ["This is where your previous Graph basics + Union Find learning becomes useful", "The full graph family: Graph DFS, Graph BFS, Grid DFS/BFS, Multi-Source BFS, Topological Sort, Union Find, Dijkstra, 0-1 BFS, Bellman-Ford, Floyd-Warshall, Minimum Spanning Tree, Bipartite Graph, Strongly Connected Components, DAG DP", "Same framework each time: why it exists, mental model, recognition triggers, invariant, variations, when NOT to use, combinations, complexity, follow-ups, representative problems, pattern map", "Key adjustment: graphs have much more overlap than trees, so we'll explicitly cover the decision tree for choosing DFS vs BFS vs Union-Find vs Topological Sort vs Dijkstra"] }
    ]
  }
};
